es security of moments of great  peril and danger I think this is an  illustration of the fundamental  misunderstanding about what we're trying  to leave the there's no no one on my  side of the argument is saying that we  would detach ourselves from important  international European meetings about  defense about security about  counterintelligence about working on  trying to stop mass illegal migration  but all those things that David Blunkett  described taking place in 2001 can be  done at an intergovernmental level they  do not need to be policed by  Brussels commission they do not need to  be adjudicated upon by the European  Court of Justice in Luxembourg they do  not need to be part of the rush to build  a federal European super-state  a United States of Europe which is  effectively what they are trying to  create  so all that cooperation could continuing  there are plenty of people you'll you'll  have heard from penny Mordaunt who's a  defense minister who believes our  defence would certainly not be  compromised John Hayes the security  Minister is also on the leave camp  everybody understands the need to  cooperate to protect our security what  you don't need to do is remain part of  the federal system which takes away  power from the UK Parliament power from  the people and in classic instances  being our inability to control  immigration we talked a little bit about  the economy the IFS report yesterday  which talked about short-term recession  we had a treasury forecast which I'm  sure you would have criticized at the  start of the week as well tactically  there is a census that not theirs coming  through from some people who feel the  shrewdest thing to add on from your  perspective would have been to say look  yes there's going to be a short term  here there may be some recession the  economy may go backwards for a period of  months possibly even years but that  ultimately is a price worth paying  rather than actually what's been going  going on which 